Let a control-centre row hold any number of tiles, not just four.

DragDropGrid sized every tile from a hard-coded ladder -- <=25 became a
quarter, <=50 a half -- and told each delegate `gridColumns: 4`. A width
of 20 therefore rendered as a quarter and the fifth tile wrapped, so a
five-across row could not be expressed in settings at all.

Any width that divides 100 evenly now means that many equal columns
(50 -> 2, 25 -> 4, 20 -> 5, 10 -> 10), and gridColumns follows it so the
drag-and-drop placement maths agrees with what is drawn. Widths that do
not divide evenly -- 75 is the only one shipped -- fall through to the
original ladder untouched, so existing layouts render exactly as before:
50 and 25 reach the same pixel sizes by the new path ((base - s)/2 and
(base - 3s)/4 respectively), and 100 gives the full width.

The same ladder is duplicated in utils/layout.js, which decides ROW
PACKING rather than tile size; with only the delegate patched, five 20%
tiles drew at a fifth each and still wrapped after four. Both get the
same rule.

Applied to the installed QML rather than as a `patches` entry: the
derivation's src is the Go backend (source/core), while the shell lives
in source/quickshell, so a source patch would have nothing to apply to.

